Step 5 — Create the input file

* nano input.txt


Type:
hello hadoop
hello world
hadoop mapreduce
hello hadoop


Save using Ctrl + O → Enter → Ctrl + X.

Verify:
* cat input.txt


The four lines should display.
Step 6 — Start Spark
* spark-shell --master local[*]


Wait until you see:
scala>


local[*] means Spark runs locally using the available processor cores.
Step 7 — Load the input file
At the scala> prompt, enter:
* val inputfile = sc.textFile("file:///home/tce/spark_lab/input.txt")


Check the input:
* inputfile.collect().foreach(println)


Your four input lines should appear.
Step 8 — Execute WordCount
Enter this as one line:
* val counts = inputfile.flatMap(line => line.split("\\s+")).map(word => (word, 1)).reduceByKey(_ + _)


Display the counts:
* counts.collect().foreach(println)


